            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous rupture of a functioning adrenocortical carcinoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>974-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3349/ymj.2010.51.6.974</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a rare malignancy with poor prognosis, and it can be classified as either a functional or nonfunctional tumor. Affected patients usually present with abdominal pain or with symptoms related to the mass effect or hormonal activity of the tumor. Several cases of spontaneously ruptured nonfunctional adrenocortical carcinoma have been reported, but no case of a spontaneous rupture of functioning adrenocortical carcinoma has been described. We report a functioning adrenocortical carcinoma that spontaneously ruptured during a work-up.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
